[LESSON]: Can't use 'if-then-else' in Alloy 6 facts → Must use implication (=>) and disjunction (or) for conditional logic.
[LESSON]: When quantifying over requests, ensure that quantifiers do not create contradictions between mutually exclusive properties for the same request.
[LESSON]: Alloy 6 requires careful use of quantification logic—overly strong "all" constraints can easily make a model UNSAT; use "some" and "or" to provide liveness.
[LESSON]: Group-processing facts must be written so that for any pending set, at least one of the disjuncts (High, Medium, Low, or none) can apply, and not exclude all possibilities.
[LESSON]: Alloy 6 will not report which conjunct makes a fact UNSAT—incremental commenting and re-enabling is necessary for diagnosis.
